/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  CARBON DEPOSITION IN A STEAM REFORMER -- a Gibbs reactor with a PURE SOLID.

  Two reformers see the same methane at 1000 K and 1 bar; only the
  steam-to-carbon ratio differs:

        reformerLow   S/C = 0.8   (steam-lean: the reformer COKES)
        reformerHigh  S/C = 3.0   (a steam margin: no carbon -- the same
                                   feed and conditions as gibbs02)

  Six species over three elements (C H O), so R = N - M = 3 independent
  reactions.  A convenient set is

        CH4 + H2O  <->  CO + 3 H2       (reforming)
        CO  + H2O  <->  CO2 + H2        (water-gas shift)
        CH4        <->  C(gr) + 2 H2    (methane cracking)

  and the Boudouard reaction, 2 CO <-> C(gr) + CO2, is a combination of
  the three (cracking - reforming - shift, rearranged), so it adds nothing.
  A Gibbs reactor needs no reaction list at all: the element balance is
  the whole constraint.

  GRAPHITE IS NOT A GAS.  Its record says `referenceState pureSolid;`, so the
  reactor gives it its OWN phase: activity 1, chemical potential equal to
  its pure-solid standard potential, amount >= 0.  The reactor first solves
  the gas WITHOUT the solid and reads the carbon activity there,

        a_C = exp( pi_C - g_C(gr)/RT )     (pi_C: the carbon element potential)
            = K_cracking * p_CH4 / p_H2^2  (partial pressures in bar),

  a_C > 1 means depositing carbon LOWERS the Gibbs energy, so the solid
  appears and the gas is re-equilibrated to a_C = 1; a_C <= 1 means it
  does not.  Both verdicts are printed with the activity that decided them
  and published as KPIs (n_solid_graphite_mol_s, activity_graphite).

  Expected, recomputed INDEPENDENTLY by bin/curate/check_gibbs_solid_phase.py
  from a reaction-based formulation on this case's own records:
      S/C 0.8:  a_C (carbon-free) = 2.33 -> about 0.25 mol graphite / mol CH4
      S/C 3.0:  a_C (carbon-free) = 0.08 -> no graphite; gibbs02's answer.

  The deposit leaves in the reactor's outlet as its SOLID phase, priced on
  the solid rung by the energy report and counted by the mass and element
  balances -- a coke deposit does not leave by a pipe of its own.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

// stream state lives on disk in 0/ (per-stream files); flowsheetDict is TOPOLOGY only

units
(
    {
        name        reformerLow;
        type        gibbsReactor;
        in          feedLow;
        outputs     ( syngasLow );

        operation
        {
            T          1000.0 K;
            P          1.0 bar;

            elements   ( C  H  O );

            species
            (
                { name  CH4;       atoms  ( 1  4  0 ); }
                { name  water;     atoms  ( 0  2  1 ); }
                { name  CO;        atoms  ( 1  0  1 ); }
                { name  CO2;       atoms  ( 1  0  2 ); }
                { name  H2;        atoms  ( 0  2  0 ); }
                { name  graphite;  atoms  ( 1  0  0 ); }
            );
        }
    }
    {
        name        reformerHigh;
        type        gibbsReactor;
        in          feedHigh;
        outputs     ( syngasHigh );

        operation
        {
            T          1000.0 K;
            P          1.0 bar;

            elements   ( C  H  O );

            species
            (
                { name  CH4;       atoms  ( 1  4  0 ); }
                { name  water;     atoms  ( 0  2  1 ); }
                { name  CO;        atoms  ( 1  0  1 ); }
                { name  CO2;       atoms  ( 1  0  2 ); }
                { name  H2;        atoms  ( 0  2  0 ); }
                { name  graphite;  atoms  ( 1  0  0 ); }
            );
        }
    }
);
